---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import PageIntro from '../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import { PAGE_HERO_IMAGES } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
   
    description="A concise digest of leading clinical negligence decisions concerning radiological interpretation, incidental findings, expert evidence and imaging pathways."
>
    <PageHero
        eyebrow="Radiology legal cases"
       
        description="Leading decisions on diagnostic interpretation, incidental findings, expert evidence and imaging pathways."
        heroImage={PAGE_HERO_IMAGES["/radiology-legal-cases"] || undefined}
    />


    <PageIntro
        eyebrow="Case law"
        paragraphs={[
            'A selective digest of decisions relevant to radiological practice. Each entry identifies the issue, material facts and legal principle.',
            'Appellate decisions carry greater precedential weight than first-instance judgments. More detailed commentary on recent cases appears in Recent Developments.'
        ]}
        contents={[
            { href: '#penney', label: 'Penney (2000)' },
            { href: '#muller', label: 'Muller (2017)' },
            { href: '#darnley', label: 'Darnley (2018)' },
            { href: '#kjy', label: 'KJY (2023)' },
            { href: '#spellman', label: 'Spellman (2024)' },
            { href: '#themes', label: 'Principles' },
        ]}
        audience="Written for radiologists, solicitors, barristers and expert witnesses."
        reviewed="October 2026"
    />


    <section class="band-paper">
        <div class="container-page min-w-0">
            <div class="doc min-w-0">
                <Figure
                    src="/images/spine_CT.jpg"
                    alt="Cross-sectional CT image of the spine"
                    label="Plate 01"
                    caption="The image is evidence, not the legal conclusion"
                    subtitle="The court must determine what the original study showed before deciding whether the report fell below the required standard."
                    ratio="wide"
                />
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band-white">
        <div class="container-page min-w-0 space-y-12">

            

            

            

            
            <Section
                id="penney"
                index="03"
                eyebrow="Diagnostic screening"
               
                title="Penney"
                citation="[2000] Lloyd's Rep Med 41 (CA)"
            >
                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Issue:</strong> The standard applicable to false-negative cervical
                    screening reports.
                </p>

                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Facts:</strong> Cervical cytology slides were reported as negative.
                    The court found abnormalities that should have led to a different
                    classification and further action.
                </p>

                <div class="rule-quote">
                    <p class="text-caption">
                        <strong>Principle:</strong> the court must determine what the material
                        showed. Evidence that other screeners might also have issued a negative
                        report was not conclusive where the abnormalities required a different
                        response.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="note-box">
                    <p>
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Relevance:</strong>
                        Penney concerned cytology rather than radiology. It is relevant by
                        analogy where a claim turns on whether an abnormal feature was
                        demonstrably present and whether its omission was professionally
                        defensible.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </Section>

            <Section
                id="muller"
                index="04"
                eyebrow="Diagnostic interpretation"
               
                title="Muller"
                citation="[2017] EWHC 128 (QB), [2017] QB 987"
            >
                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Issue:</strong> Whether a histopathologist negligently reported a
                    biopsy from a non-healing foot lesion as benign.
                </p>

                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Facts:</strong> The biopsy was reported as showing a non-malignant
                    ulcer. The lesion was subsequently diagnosed as acral lentiginous melanoma.
                </p>

                <div class="rule-quote">
                    <p class="text-caption">
                        <strong>Principle:</strong> the court considered whether the report fell
                        below the required professional standard, taking account of the slides,
                        competing expert evidence, the rarity of the condition and hindsight.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="note-box">
                    <p>
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Relevance:</strong>
                        Muller is a histopathology decision, not a radiology case. Its approach
                        to visual diagnosis is relevant by analogy but should now be read
                        alongside Brady.
                    </p>

                    <p class="mt-2">
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Causation:</strong>
                        although breach was established, damages were limited because the
                        melanoma had probably already metastasised by the date of the negligent
                        report.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </Section>

            <Section
                id="darnley"
                index="05"
                eyebrow="Imaging pathway"
               
                title="Darnley"
                citation="[2018] UKSC 50"
            >
                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Issue:</strong> Whether misleading information given by an
                    emergency-department receptionist could amount to a breach of the
                    hospital's duty of care.
                </p>

                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Facts:</strong> A patient with a head injury was incorrectly told
                    that he might wait four or five hours before being seen. He left after
                    approximately 19 minutes and later collapsed from an extradural haematoma.
                </p>

                <div class="rule-quote">
                    <p class="text-caption">
                        <strong>Principle:</strong> the hospital owed a duty not to provide
                        misleading information about the likely timing of clinical assessment.
                        The receptionist's non-clinical role did not prevent liability for
                        inaccurate information given within the scope of that role.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="note-box">
                    <p>
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Radiology relevance:</strong>
                        Darnley was not a reporting case. Its relevance lies in the diagnostic
                        pathway: administrative information and operational systems may
                        determine whether urgent assessment and imaging occur.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </Section>

            <Section
                id="kjy"
                index="06"
                eyebrow="Incidental abnormality"
               
                title="KJY"
                citation="[2023] EWHC 2719 (KB)"
            >
                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Issue:</strong> Whether radiologists reporting neonatal abdominal
                    radiographs should have identified hip abnormalities incidental to the
                    clinical question.
                </p>

                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Facts:</strong> Abdominal radiographs were obtained shortly after
                    birth because of choking and swallowing difficulties. Bilateral
                    developmental dysplasia of the hips was diagnosed later. Abnormal hip
                    alignment was visible on the earlier radiographs but had not been reported.
                </p>

                <div class="rule-quote">
                    <p class="text-caption">
                        <strong>Principle:</strong> an abnormality is not necessarily outside
                        the reporter's responsibility merely because it is incidental to the
                        referral question. On the facts, the hip abnormality should have been
                        identified and reported.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="note-box">
                    <p>
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Significance:</strong>
                        the clinical question focuses the examination but does not invariably
                        limit responsibility to a single structure. Visibility, clinical
                        importance and the reporting circumstances remain relevant.
                    </p>

                    <p class="mt-2">
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Status:</strong>
                        KJY is a first-instance decision. It should not be treated as creating
                        an absolute rule detached from its facts and expert evidence.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
                    <a href="/recent-developments/kjy-2023-whole-image-review">
                        Detailed KJY commentary &rarr;
                    </a>
                </p>
            </Section>

            <Section
                id="spellman"
                index="07"
                eyebrow="MRI interpretation"
               
                title="Spellman"
                citation="[2024] EWHC 2011 (KB)"
            >
                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Issue:</strong> Whether an MRI was negligently reported as showing
                    only mild stenosis when it was alleged to demonstrate cauda equina
                    compression requiring urgent decompression.
                </p>

                <p class="para">
                    <strong>Facts:</strong> The claimant had previous spinal surgery, chronic
                    pain and longstanding urological symptoms. She alleged that the reporting
                    radiologist had failed to identify cauda equina compression on MRI.
                </p>

                <div class="rule-quote">
                    <p class="text-caption">
                        <strong>Principle:</strong> the court first determines what the imaging
                        showed and then considers whether the report was reasonably open to a
                        competent radiologist. Descriptions such as mild or moderate may
                        involve legitimate judgment where no fixed grading system exists.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div class="note-box">
                    <p>
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Outcome:</strong>
                        the court found no radiological evidence of cauda equina compression.
                        The report was reasonably open to a competent radiologist and the claim
                        was dismissed.
                    </p>

                    <p class="mt-2">
                        <strong class="text-slate-800">Significance:</strong>
                        disagreement between experts does not establish breach. Objective image
                        content must be distinguished from reasonable differences in
                        description and interpretation.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
                    <a href="/recent-developments/spellman-2024-radiological-opinion">
                        Detailed Spellman commentary &rarr;
                    </a>
                </p>
            </Section>

            <Section
                id="themes"
                index="08"
                eyebrow="Synthesis"
                title="Principles Emerging from the Cases"
               
            >
                <div class="entry-list mt-0">
                    <div class="entry">
                        <h3 class="entry-title">Determine what the study showed</h3>
                        <p class="para">
                            The content of the original examination must be established before
                            the adequacy of the report can be assessed.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="entry">
                        <h3 class="entry-title">Diagnostic reporting involves judgment</h3>
                        <p class="para">
                            Bolam and Bolitho apply. A later diagnosis does not make an earlier
                            reasonable interpretation negligent.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="entry">
                        <h3 class="entry-title">Assess the contemporaneous context</h3>
                        <p class="para">
                            Referral details, protocol, image quality, prior examinations and
                            contemporary knowledge are all relevant.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="entry">
                        <h3 class="entry-title">Consider significant incidental findings</h3>
                        <p class="para">
                            A clearly visible and clinically important abnormality may require
                            reporting despite falling outside the principal clinical question.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="entry">
                        <h3 class="entry-title">Preserve expert independence</h3>
                        <p class="para">
                            Relevant professional relationships must be disclosed. Unsupported
                            or partisan opinion may be rejected.
                        </p>
                    </div>

                    <div class="entry">
                        <h3 class="entry-title">Separate breach from causation</h3>
                        <p class="para">
                            A negligent report does not establish that every subsequent injury
                            was caused by it. The clinical counterfactual must be proved.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </Section>
        

        </div>
    </section>
</BaseLayout>